00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:46.583
And now let's get back to
Cyprus to talk about donkeys.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:52.005
I'm in Cyprus. And I am
looking at a lot of donkeys.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:55.088
(upbeat funky music)

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:00.380
All over this island,
there are tons of donkeys.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.653
They're just everywhere.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.670
These donkeys don't belong to anyone.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.890
They don't have a farm
that they're working.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.100
They don't have a home besides the wild.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.490
They stop traffic. They're on the road.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:19.747
They just don't care.

00:03:19.747 --> 00:03:22.170
They just,
(Johnny laughs)

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.330
they're just so stubborn

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.377
and they just sit there
and they're just like,

00:03:24.377 --> 00:03:25.857
"Hey, cool. You're a car."

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.587
Inevitably comes the question.

00:03:29.587 --> 00:03:30.735
"What are these donkeys?

00:03:30.735 --> 00:03:33.176
And how on earth did they get here?"

00:03:33.176 --> 00:03:35.000
Answer to that question,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.922
like every other part of this series,

00:03:36.922 --> 00:03:39.520
has to do with the conflict.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.890
- [Reporter] Tanks of the Turkish army

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.402
on the outskirts of Famagusta,

00:03:42.402 --> 00:03:45.610
are about to complete
their victory in Cyprus.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:48.963
They will take what they want
to Cypress by force of arms.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.610
- [Johnny] So we're now in
episode three of this series,

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:53.778
and hopefully you're familiar

00:03:53.778 --> 00:03:56.660
with the story of this island by now.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.380
To summarize, in 1974,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.660
the Turkish government invaded
the Northern part of Cyprus.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:02.493
In that process,

00:04:02.493 --> 00:04:04.831
all of the Greek Cypriots
who lived in the north fled

00:04:04.831 --> 00:04:07.720
to safety in the south and
all of the Turkish Cypriots

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.520
in the south fled to safety in the north.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.290
The Greek Cypriots who were up here

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.360
fled and left their farms empty.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.610
Their homes, empty.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.080
Their churches, empty.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:19.924
But they didn't take
the donkeys with them.

00:04:19.924 --> 00:04:22.500
(funky music)

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.840
There weren't that many at that point,

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.650
but the donkeys started
wandering the countryside,

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.220
finding food.
(donkey braying)

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.870
Then they started mating,

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.480
not having a natural predator of any kind,

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.690
so they started proliferating.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:38.030
Classic invasive species type situation.

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.930
So modern day Cyprus is
a pretty advanced place,

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:46.930
but not too long ago, these
places were agrarian societies.

00:04:46.939 --> 00:04:49.610
(ethereal music)

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.680
For thousands of years, Cyprus
has been the home to farming.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.219
To a serious amount of farming.

00:04:55.219 --> 00:04:56.950
- [Reporter] Wherever
there is water and earth,

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:00.020
there the Cypriots find
there's food and livelihood.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.630
- Some of the best olive
oil production on earth

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.300
historically has happened here in Cyprus.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.170
- [Second Reporter] They've grown olives

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:10.020
and all sorts of fruit
in Cyprus for longer

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.410
than anyone has written harsh history.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:14.190
- So I'm trying to read my lines, guys,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.200
for this video I'm making,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.350
so could you just sort
of give me a minute?

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.993
Could you give me a minute? Thanks.

00:05:18.993 --> 00:05:20.592
- [Announcer] And these
are the recently introduced

00:05:20.592 --> 00:05:22.040
John Deere 55 Series.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.370
- [Johnny] Even in the 1970s,

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.760
when tractors were
normalized around the world,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.280
owning a tractor in Cyprus
was actually very difficult.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.470
This was an island in the Middle East.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.150
It was difficult to import stuff here.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:36.930
So the farmers stuck to
what they had been doing

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:38.930
for literally thousands of years,

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.940
using animals to do their farm work.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.300
Horses were expensive
and not very abundant.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.660
And so donkeys were the answer.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.460
In 1974, when the Turks invaded the north,

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.050
it was estimated that
there were two donkeys

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.270
on average for every farming household.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.840
And with one third of the
island's population being farmers,

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.410
that means a lot of donkeys.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.210
Donkeys were the powerhouse
that would help pull plows,

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.613
or run mills that would make olive oil.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.300
So after this mass exodus from the north,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.330
these donkeys were left abandoned,
orphaned by the conflict.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:19.110
So, there's donkeys everywhere.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.930
Why is that a problem again?

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.350
Is that a problem?

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.140
Well, it wasn't a
problem for a while until

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:25.810
the donkeys proliferated so much

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.000
that there ended up
being thousands of them

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.280
here in Northern Cyprus.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.520
It started to become an
issue when the donkeys

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.690
started showing up to farms

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.250
and pulling up all of the
crops and eating them.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.080
It's like the revenge of the donkeys.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.870
They're like, "You left us out
to dry when it was war time.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.340
We're coming back to get revenge."

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.500
No, but seriously, this
became like a real issue

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.737
for the farmers in this
region who were just like,

00:06:50.737 --> 00:06:54.848
"I don't need wild donkeys
messing with my crops."

00:06:54.848 --> 00:06:57.348
(funky music)

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.467
So the natural response
for a lot of people was,

00:07:00.467 --> 00:07:02.300
"Why don't we just hunt these donkeys

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.490
and just thin out the
population by hunting them."

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.480
But after one group of
donkeys was found dead,

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.070
there was a big backlash.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:12.790
And in fact, a backlash
that brought Greek Cypriots

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:15.157
and Turkish Cypriots together
in an unlikely moment

00:07:15.157 --> 00:07:18.950
of collaboration and cooperation.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:22.080
Animal rights activists on
both sides of the border

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.690
got together and lobbied for a law

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.630
that would make it
illegal to kill donkeys,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.670
and in fact, would come with
a seven year prison penalty,

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.020
if you killed a donkey.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.760
if you have a hard time understanding

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.000
why these donkeys have protected status

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.460
and why you'll go to jail for
seven years if you kill one,

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.790
that's because you don't understand

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.644
Cyprus' connection to
donkeys, and neither did I.

00:07:43.644 --> 00:07:46.977
(gentle spirited music)

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.070
As well as being an integral part

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.700
to this Cypriot agricultural
economy for thousands of years,

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.720
donkeys were also used as a status symbol.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.790
They were exchanged for land.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.790
And when the British occupied this island

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:02.150
during World War II,

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:06.870
they used donkeys to transport
weapons and ammunition.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:10.020
Cleopatra used to bathe in donkey milk,

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.820
which was like a very superstitious thing.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.366
And people still believe

00:08:15.366 --> 00:08:18.240
that it has some sort
of medical properties,

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.943
and so they make medicinal
supplements out of donkey milk.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.090
The Cyprus donkey has been on this island

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:26.550
for thousands of years,

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.092
long before machines ever came
along to replace their job,

00:08:30.092 --> 00:08:34.180
long before the borders shifted
and divided this island.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.380
And yes, while it seems
like there's a large number

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.420
of these Cyprus donkeys here in the north,

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:42.150
a recent survey found that there
are only around a thousand.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:45.370
Which is far less than the
giant pandas in the world.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.160
It's less than the amount of
black rhinos in the world,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.580
and it's less than the amount

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.193
of the Sumatran orangutans in the world.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:55.743
So there are real
reasons why these donkeys

00:08:55.743 --> 00:08:59.830
are being saved and why you'll
go to jail if you kill one.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.550
There's simply not very
many left in the world.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.420
Which again, feels a little bit hard

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.683
to tell when you're here.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.730
But more than being endangered,

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:12.960
I think the real reason why
Cypriots, both Greek and Turk,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.790
are so protective of these animals,

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.530
is because they represent to them

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.173
a time when this island
wasn't split in two.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.735
A time when everyone
here were just Cypriots.

00:09:24.735 --> 00:09:26.560
In the same way that these donkeys

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.030
are not Greek donkeys or Turkish donkeys,

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.203
they're just Cypriot donkeys.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.020
And yes, it's also because
they're really cute.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.710
I mean, they are kind
of beautiful animals.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:43.250
Like I've never taken a
moment to think about donkeys

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:44.890
and look at them.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:49.890
But they are like really
kind of striking animals.

00:09:50.128 --> 00:09:53.461
(gentle ethereal music)

